  "query_preview": "..Forecast remains on track for an area of high\npressure to build eastward into the region over the next few days,\nbringing increasing heat areawide with an influx of moisture to\nfollow. Across Utah and southwest Wyoming, the increasing\ntemperature trend is expected to peak on Friday/ Saturday, with\ntemperatures slowly decreasing through the weekend and into early\nnext week. Along the Wasatch Front and remainder of the I-15\ncorridor through Cedar City, high temperatures will be back into\nthe upper-90s (Cedar City area) and the low-100s in the Salt Lake\nCity, Ogden, and Provo area. By noon on Friday, a series of heat\nadvisories and extreme heat warnings will go into effect across\nnorthern Utah, with existing warnings and advisories in southern\nUtah remaining in effect.\n\nMid-level moisture will remain in place across the southern half\nof Utah, as was the case today. As was evident with today, high-\nbased showers and thunderstorms will bring potential for strong\noutflow winds in excess of 40-45 mph. With mid-level moisture\nspreading a skosh farther northward, would anticipate coverage of\nthese showers to extend into Millard County Friday afternoon.\nThough a majority of these showers and thunderstorms will be\nlargely dry, little steering flow in the lower to middle portions\nof the atmosphere will allow for slow moving storms, thus bringing\npotential for isolated heavy rainfall in near-stationary storms.\nWhile the threat of flash flooding in the typical areas is quite\nlow, it is not zero.\n\nThe lower levels of the atmosphere will remain fairly dry through\nthe weekend until the center of the high pressure positions itself\nover the Four Corners region, which will help to advect an influx\nof lower level moisture into the forecast area. Given the current\nmodel guidance, would anticipate this to occur around the mid-week\ntimeframe (Wednesday/ Thursday), with high-based largely dry\nshowers and thunderstorms each afternoon until then. This pattern\nlooks to remain solidly in place thereafter.",
